### 1. Add Required NuGet Package

```bash
dotnet add package Microsoft.Extensions.Resilience
```

### 2. Building a Resilience Pipeline with Circuit Breaker

You can register a named pipeline at startup (e.g., in your DI configuration) for any kind of operation, not just HTTP. The operation could be database access, file IO, or any business logic.

**Example: Registering the pipeline**

```csharp
using Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection;
using Polly;
using Polly.CircuitBreaker;

var services = new ServiceCollection();

services.AddResiliencePipeline("my-circuit-breaker-pipeline", builder =>
{
    builder.AddCircuitBreaker(new CircuitBreakerStrategyOptions
    {
        FailureRatio = 0.5, // Break if more than 50% fail
        SamplingDuration = TimeSpan.FromSeconds(10), // In a 10 second window
        MinimumThroughput = 8, // Minimum 8 operations must complete before calculating failure ratio
        BreakDuration = TimeSpan.FromSeconds(30), // Break for 30 seconds
        ShouldHandle = new PredicateBuilder().Handle<Exception>() // Handle any exception (customize as needed)
    });
});
```
